{
  "format": "TaleWeaver",
  "version": "1.3",
  "adventure": {
    "origin_id": "3e08c00f-55f1-4d3e-880e-e4e3b5083ba9",
    "title": "El Profesor's Purgatory",
    "teaser": "Three portals. One disappointed teacher. Your escape depends on verbs you slept through.",
    "cover_appearance": "surreal classroom, glowing neon portals, stern Spanish teacher, floating verb charts, teal and amber",
    "version": "3.0.0",
    "creator": "Jean Schmitz",
    "copyright": "Copyright (c) 2026 by Jean Schmitz",
    "license": "CC BY-NC 4.0",
    "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/",
    "language": "English",
    "original_prompt": "You are trapped in a surreal classroom with a deeply unimpressed Spanish teacher, and the only way to pass the class is to survive absurd portal hopping real life scenarios armed only with your questionable vocabulary.\n\nPlot Summary\nYou fell asleep in Spanish 101. Now you are locked in Room 404, supervised by Señor Valderrama, a man whose disappointed sigh can wither a cactus. The classroom walls are lined with glowing portals. Each one drops you into a hyper specific, slightly unhinged real life simulation where your Spanish skills dictate your survival. You must haggle at a chaotic fish market where the vendors have zero patience for bad accents. You have to navigate the bureaucratic nightmare of a Madrid post office to mail a simple package. You even need to survive a dramatic telenovela wedding where you are suddenly the center of a love triangle.\n\nCompleting the mini tasks in each portal earns you a 'Sello de Aprobación' (Badge of Approval). You must bring these badges back to the classroom and present them to Valderrama. He will offer devastatingly witty critiques of your performance. If you collect every badge and satisfy his impossible standards, you get to wake up. If you fail, you are doomed to conjugate irregular verbs until the end of time.\n\nCore Pillars\nGenre: Educational Comedy / Portal Fantasy\n\nTone: Dry, cynical humor with high stakes absurdity\n\nPlayer Role: A chronically unprepared student desperate to escape eternal summer school\n\nMain Conflict: Your terrible grasp of grammar versus chaotic real world interactions\n\nAntagonistic Force: Señor Valderrama's relentless grading scale and the highly judgmental NPCs inside the portals\n\nDistinctive World Rules:\n\nLiteral Translations Fail: NPCs take direct English translations literally, causing hilarious and dangerous misunderstandings.\n\nGrammar as a Weapon: Correctly conjugating a verb functions like a conversational trump card to bypass obstacles.\n\nThe Chalkboard Hub: All portals safely spit you back into the classroom, but you must endure Valderrama's sarcastic feedback before trying again.\n\nKey Locations: Room 404 (The Hub), El Mercado de Mariscos (Fish Market), The Telenovela Church, La Oficina de Correos (Post Office)\n\nFactions: The Perfectionists (former students trapped forever who act as hall monitors), The Locals (portal NPCs who refuse to speak English)\n\nSignature NPC Ideas: Señor Valderrama (the perpetually bored teacher sipping cold coffee), Abuela Carmen (a vendor who haggles aggressively and insults your posture), Alejandro (a telenovela groom demanding perfect subjunctive tense)\n\nMain Quest Seeds: Acquire the 'Sello de Supervivencia' from the Fish Market by buying a specific red snapper without starting a riot. Obtain the 'Sello de Drama' by objecting to the wedding before the priest finishes his sentence.\n\nSide Quest Seeds: Bribe the trapped students with smuggled churros to acquire conjugation cheat sheets. Find Valderrama's lost red grading pen inside the labyrinthine Post Office.\n\nWin Condition: Collect all portal badges, present them to Valderrama, and survive his final oral exam to wake up from the nightmare.\n\nLose Condition: Fail the tasks completely, get a giant red \"F\" stamped on your forehead, and become a permanent member of The Perfectionists.\n\nWhy It Works\nThe hub structure provides a clear, satisfying gameplay loop built around learning and immediate application.\n\nSeñor Valderrama serves as a brilliant anchor character, offering harsh but genuinely funny feedback that motivates the player to do better.\n\nUsing real life situations with a chaotic twist makes the educational aspect feel like a genuine adventure rather than a textbook quiz.",
    "image_url": "assets/el-profesors-purgatory-ar-66ymz4e4_cover_1b225390.jpg",
