    "plot": "After dozing off in Spanish 101, you awaken in a surreal dimensional version of Room 404 overseen by Señor Valderrama, whose disappointed sigh can curdle milk. Three shimmering portals lead to hyper-specific, chaotic Spanish simulations: a cutthroat coastal fish market, a bureaucratic Madrid post office, and a high-melodrama telenovela wedding. To escape eternal summer school and wake up at your desk, you must navigate each simulation, earn its 'Sello de Aprobación' (Badge of Approval) by solving puzzles and deploying proper grammar, combine the three badges into a complete Approval Portfolio, and unlock the final exit back to reality.",
    "rules": "1. Literal Translations Fail: Using false cognates (like 'embarazada' for embarrassed) causes comedic setbacks. 2. Grammar as a Key: Puzzle codes and switches rely on Spanish vocabulary, verb conjugations, and counting clues across portals. 3. Non-Lethal Purgatory: Combat is disabled; obstacles are overcome via puzzle-solving, item combinations, and social persuasion.",
    "intro_text": "The fluorescent lights of Room 404 hum in a minor key that feels personal. You blink against the glare, peeling your cheek off a laminated desk blotter. The clock on the wall has no hands, just the word 'SIEMPRE' painted across its face. At the front of the room stands Señor Valderrama, swirling a mug of pitch-black, ice-cold coffee. He doesn't look angry; he looks worse. He looks disappointed.\n\nAlong the cinderblock walls, where motivational posters about regular '-ar' verbs used to hang, three glowing dimensional rifts pulse with unnatural energy. From one comes the briny stench of raw snapper and the chaotic shouting of a mercado; from another, the rhythmic thud of bureaucratic rubber stamps; from the third, a dramatic telenovela organ chord that refuses to resolve.\n\nValderrama takes a slow sip, tosses a glowing pocket dictionary onto your desk, and gestures toward the portals with a piece of chalk. \"Since my lecture bored you into a coma, Señor Vance, welcome to immersion by fire. Out there, nobody speaks a word of English. You will have to translate, conjugate, and talk your way through three real-world trials in actual Spanish. Bring me three Sellos de Aprobación, or get comfortable writing verb tables until the sun burns out. ¿Comprendes?\"",
    "completed_condition": "",
    "gameover_condition": "",
